The '''''Treaty of Anping''''' was an agreement concluded at [[Anping]] in 1538 between the [[Zong colonial administration]] and the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina state]] of the Lurandian interior. It recognised the Hahkina as a sovereign polity, renounced Zong territorial claims to the interior of the mainland, established terms of trade and arms supply, and provided for a joint military campaign against the [[mahik]] chiefdoms to the north, whose lands were to be divided between the two parties.

The treaty is cited as the first instance of a New World state being recognised as sovereign by an Old World power. It is also regarded as the clearest early expression of the doctrine that governed Zong's Lurandian colonies for the remainder of their existence — that the Zong presence was to be maintained through a small number of defensible coastal settlements rather than through the government of territory.

The treaty was concluded in the same year as the founding of [[Tamsui]], and the two are generally treated together: Tamsui provided the harbour that the Zong required, and the treaty supplied the diplomatic conditions in which it could be held.

The treaty is cited as the first recognition of a New World state by an Old World state. No earlier agreement between a Fosian or Illypnian colonial power and an indigenous American polity is known to have treated the indigenous party as sovereign.

== Background ==

=== Zong colonial position ===

By the 1530s the Zong colonial presence in the New World had grown beyond the capacity of the colonial administration to govern it. The [[Louyang]] colony, centred on Anping, had been established in 1474 and had expanded across much of the western seaboard, largely at the cost of sustained warfare with the peoples of the interior.

Zong policy toward indigenous resistance had settled by this period into a combination of military demonstration and diplomatic accommodation. The court had concluded from the failure of [[Xinhai]] — the first Zong colony, established in 1456 and reduced to a fortified trading post within two decades — that sustained conflict at the end of a supply line reaching across the [[Shendan Ocean]] was not sustainable.

The administration consequently concluded that permanent territorial rule of the mainland interior was not achievable, and that the Zong presence should instead be secured through a small number of defensible coastal settlements. Under this doctrine each settlement was to be a discrete holding of bounded extent — termed a territorial island in the colonial correspondence — surrounded by territory left to indigenous government.

=== Hahkina ===

The Hahkina were an agrarian state on the great plain south of the mahik lands, organised around a king and a regnal council, and the largest and most centralised indigenous polity the Zong had encountered in the region.

Contact between the Hahkina and Zong shipping dated from 1458, when navigators charting the coast recorded the presence of substantial fisheries along their shore. For approximately eighty years this contact remained commercial and intermittent. Zong records indicate that the Hahkina came to regard the Zong as a distant power with whom an understanding existed, a view that the colonial administration did not share and did not contradict.

By the 1530s the Hahkina had acquired firearms and light artillery from colonial traders, largely without the authorisation of the colonial government. These weapons gave them a decided advantage over the upland peoples to their south and were used in their long-running war with the mahik chiefdoms to the north.

=== Mahik peoples ===

The lands north of the Hahkina contained no single people and no state. They were occupied by approximately seven chiefdoms, speaking related but distinct languages, with no common government and no established diplomatic practice.

The Hahkina termed these peoples [[mahik|''mahik'']], "kingless" — a pejorative, applied to peoples who lacked a ruler of the kind the Hahkina recognised, and used also of the Chidoha and Salowi peoples of the west. Zong records of the period do not distinguish between the chiefdoms, and treat them as a single population. Later scholarship inherited this treatment, and applies to them collectively the name [[Kawesa]], coined from the name of one chiefdom, Akwesa.

One chiefdom, Akwesa, held the site of the future Tamsui. Its members had dealt with Zong traders from 1511 and with the colonial administration from the 1530s, and it is the only chiefdom whose name the colonial record preserves.

