Emperor Tiancheng reigned from 1521 to 1560. The founding of Tamsui (1538), the First Mahik War (1538–1542) and the colonial split of 1544 all fall within his reign.

Ông Bûn-hêng held the office of Governor-General of the Zong overseas colonies at Anping, responsible for the whole of the western Lurandian territory before the administrative division of 1544. He received the Hahkina embassy, concluded the treaty, and authorised the campaign that followed.

Tsulhachi was king of the Hahkina at the time of the treaty. His name is a regnal one, derived from ''tsula'', "lord", and ''hachin'', "centre" — the same root that supplies the ethnonym Hachinawa and the name of the empire, Hachinawa.

Nachiha, his son, led the embassy to Anping — the first member of the Hahkina royal house recorded as having left Lurandia — and later succeeded his father as king.

== Negotiation ==

=== Origin of the alliance ===

The treaty was the result of a deliberate policy decision rather than of incidental contact. The colonial records indicate that the administration at Anping had determined, some months before any approach was made, to seek an envoy and an alliance with one of the indigenous polities — a departure from the practice of the preceding eight decades, during which Zong shipping had navigated the coast without systematic diplomacy.

The standing of the Zong to conclude an agreement affecting the northern chiefdoms rested on a claim advanced during the negotiations. The administration held that the chiefdoms were not independent polities but subjects in rebellion, on the ground that [[Hokta]], the miko of the Akwesa, had been King of the Northern Mahiks and had granted the land to the [[Sūn-heng Hâng]], which had conveyed it to the colony in 1535. On that argument the lands concerned were already the colony's, and the treaty disposed of territory it held rather than of the territory of other powers.

The claim was devised by [[Tân Bûn-khéng]] (陳文慶), the administration's negotiator, and it served an immediate practical purpose in the negotiation. The two allies required a boundary between the territory each would hold, and no natural line divided the region. The line adopted was the extent of the lands attributed to Hokta as king. A title covering the Akwesa alone would have given the Zong the Akwesa's coast only; a title covering the whole northern region gave the Zong the whole coast, and reduced the portion left to the Hahkina to the lands beyond it. The boundary of 1538 therefore follows a claim about a dead man's jurisdiction rather than anything the Zong had settled or fought for at the time.

The Hahkina accepted the arrangement. Their interest lay in the land and not in the argument by which it was distributed: the proposed line gave them a territory on their northern frontier, which was the object of the campaign, and the question of who had been king of the northern chiefdoms was of no consequence to them. Nothing in the Hahkina record indicates that the posthumous title was communicated to them, or that the claim was understood as anything other than the Zong's own account of what it held. It is likely that the Hahkina neither knew of the title nor knew who Hokta had been. No objection to the claim is recorded from any quarter, and the colonial papers give no indication that its accuracy was examined. The title was an instrument of the negotiation, devised to supply a ground of jurisdiction and a boundary line, and it appears to have been treated as such by those who made use of it.

The title was conferred on Hokta after his death and is not attested in any document of his lifetime. No chiefdom of the north is recorded as having acknowledged his authority.

The Hahkina were selected as the appropriate party. They were the largest and best-organised polity in the region, possessed institutions capable of concluding an agreement that would be observed, and had spent decades purchasing Zong weapons, indicating both an existing relationship and a demand for what the Zong could supply.

=== Mission ===

An envoy was dispatched to the Hahkina court, accompanied by interpreters — the first recorded instance of formal interpretation between the two peoples. The party carried gifts, in accordance with the conventions of Zong diplomacy.

The proposal presented to the king included provision for a military campaign. The division of the mahik lands was a subject of the negotiation rather than a consequence of it: the two parties discussed the partition of the territory in advance and agreed terms for its conquest.

King Tsulhachi received the proposal favourably, and the conclusion of the agreement was regarded at the Hahkina court as auspicious. The Hahkina had sought the mahik lands for generations; the Zong offered the means of obtaining them, and requested in return only a harbour on the coast, in which the Hahkina had no interest.

=== Embassy ===

Talks concluded with the dispatch of an embassy to Anping. Nachiha, son of King Tsulhachi, travelled to the colonial capital and was received by Governor-General Ông Bûn-hêng. The embassy bound the agreement in the persons of the two houses and acquainted the Hahkina crown prince with the power with which they had allied.

=== Authority and ratification ===

The colonial administration reported its negotiations to the government of [[Zong]] as they proceeded. Ông Bûn-hêng was granted broad authority to accomplish the objectives set, including latitude to conclude terms and commit colonial forces, subject to reporting rather than instruction.

The treaty was ratified by the government of Zong some months after its conclusion, in advance of the campaign that began on 11 August 1538. The agreement was therefore concluded through regular channels, in contrast to the arms trade that it formalised, which had previously been conducted by individual traders against the policy of the colonial government and concealed by false returns.

== Provisions ==

=== Recognition ===

The Zong colonial administration recognised the Hahkina as a sovereign state, and recognised the authority of the Hahkina king over the territory of the interior. No Zong claim to territorial sovereignty inland of the coastal settlements was asserted or maintained.

No earlier agreement between a Fosian or Illypnian colonial power and an indigenous polity is recorded as having treated the indigenous party as sovereign.

=== Limits ===

The southern limit of the territory recognised as Hahkina was fixed at the [[Tsuwasha]] (''tsu'', "south", and ''washa'', "water" — the southern river), which runs west to east across the region that later became northern [[Louyang]]. South of the Tsuwasha lay further mahik peoples, whom the Hahkina knew as the southern mahik.

The provision is the origin of the Hahkina state's defined frontier. Before the treaty the Hahkina asserted authority over the lands of their tributaries without delimitation; the agreement obliged them to accept a bounded territory with a named boundary, in the manner of a state rather than a sphere of influence.

The treaty fixed the Zong presence at defined coastal positions of bounded extent, of which the settlement at Tamsui was the principal. The colony renounced the permanent territorial occupation of the interior and undertook not to establish settlements beyond its coastal stations without agreement. In exchange, the Hahkina recognised the Zong settlements and undertook not to attack them.

=== Trade ===

The treaty established formal terms of trade, replacing the informal and largely unauthorised commerce of the preceding decades. Hahkina gold, grain and other goods were to be exchanged at the coastal settlements under the supervision of the colonial administration.

=== Arms supply ===

The treaty established a formal system of arms supply. The Zong undertook to sell weapons to the Hahkina openly and by agreement, replacing the irregular trade that had preceded it.

The concession was limited in scope. The Zong did not transfer manufacturing knowledge — the techniques of casting and boring remained within the colony and Zong — and the weapons placed on open sale were older and superseded stock, with current ordnance retained. The Hahkina therefore obtained a reliable supply of serviceable but obsolete weapons, at prices and in quantities determined by the seller, and remained dependent on Zong supply, being unable either to manufacture such weapons or to purchase current models.
== Territorial-island doctrine ==

It is also the clearest early statement of the doctrine that would govern the Zong colonies in Lurandia thereafter — that the Zong empire was a maritime and commercial power whose interests lay in harbours, trade and coastal positions rather than in the territorial government of continental interiors. The distinction between the coastal colonies and the interior, and between the Zong sphere and the indigenous sphere, defined the character of the Sinchew colony for the remainder of its existence.

== Aftermath ==

=== First Mahik War ===
{{Main|First Mahik War}}

The treaty provided for a military campaign, which began four months after its conclusion. The [[First Mahik War]] was fought from 11 August 1538 to 7 April 1542 between the allied Hahkina and Zong forces and the chiefdoms of the northern lands.

No record of deaths was kept, and no census of the chiefdoms existed against which to measure losses; the available evidence indicates that between eighty and ninety per cent of the population of the region was killed or enslaved. Survivors fled north and west into the territories of the [[Timara]] and the [[Salowi]], where they were absorbed.

The territorial outcome was the division of the region between the two allies. The north, including Tamsui and the coast, passed to the Zong and became the Sinchew colony. The south, adjoining the Hahkina frontier, passed to the Hahkina.

Akwesa, which had granted the Tamsui site to Zong traders in 1511 and whose bay had passed to the colony by purchase in 1535, had become a client of the colony before the war and survived as a minority community within the new Sinchew colony. The modern collective name for the peoples of the region, Kawesa, derives from this chiefdom's name.

=== Colonial split ===

The acquisition of the northern coast removed the dependence of the Tamsui settlement on Louyang. Two years after the conclusion of the war the colonial territory was divided along the [[Qingjiang]] river into the [[Sinchew]] and [[Louyang]] colonies. The [[Tamsui Plot]] of 1543–1544 provided the occasion for the division; the war had removed the reason for maintaining a single administration.

=== Later Mahik wars ===
The First Mahik War was the first of several campaigns conducted by the Zong colonies and their indigenous allies against the mahik peoples. A subsequent conflict, sometimes termed the Second Mahik War, was fought against the Salowi and Chidoha peoples to the west.

Historians have noted a difficulty in the naming of the peoples concerned. The collective term Kawesa, in current use, derives from Akwesa, the one chiefdom whose name and history were preserved in the colonial record — a circumstance attributable to that chiefdom's commercial relationship with the colony, and to the destruction of the remaining chiefdoms. The term thus names a population after a single one of its constituent groups.

